{"date": "2026-10-19", "post_id": 1495522, "title": "Treble Clef: Unusual X-ray Hot Radio Source", "permalink": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-19-treble-clef-unusual-x-ray-hot-radio-source/", "media_type": "image", "explanation": "<strong>Explanation:</strong> <b>Explanation</b> : What is Treble Clef? This unusual radio structure extends over 2 million light-years and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink_Panther_(character)#/media/File:Pink_Panther.png\">resembles</a> a common <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clef#Treble_clef\">musical symbol</a> -- but has its <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/09_visiblelight/\">visible light</a> hidden by our own <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-milky-way-galaxy/\">Milky Way Galaxy</a>. Examined in 2004, the nature of VLSS J0318.9+5755, <a href=\"https://chandra.si.edu/photo/2026/trebleclef/trebleclef_BU.mp4\">dubbed Treble Clef</a>, remained unknown. Recent observations by the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/chandra/\">Chandra X-ray Observatory</a> and <a href=\"https://www.mpe.mpg.de/eROSITA\">eROSITA</a>, however, have led to the <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.06659\">hypothesis</a> that the Treble Clef central cloud of charged particles was originally expelled by a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/supermassive-black-holes/\">supermassive black hole</a> at the center of a galaxy. After the radio emission from those particles faded, they may have been <a href=\"https://spacescoop.org/ar/scoops/1540/rise-of-the-radio-phoenix/\">reinvigorated</a> by a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-february-16-unexplained-shocks-around-a-white-dwarf-star/\">shock wave</a> from the collision of two <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-march-27-the-coma-cluster-of-galaxies/\">clusters of galaxies</a>. The <a href=\"https://chandra.si.edu/photo/2026/trebleclef/\">featured composite image</a> shows <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/05_radiowaves/\">radio</a> emission in red, while recently discovered <a href=\"https://chandra.harvard.edu/xray_astro/xrays.html\">X-rays</a> are shown in blue. <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/santa-claus-with-red-scarf-7sgPfAcM6_o\">Unusual objects</a> like <a href=\"https://chandra.si.edu/photo/2026/trebleclef/trebleclef_dragon.mp4\">Treble Clef</a> give humanity unexpected clues about how whole <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2026-july-26-simulation-tng50-a-galaxy-cluster-forms/\">clusters of galaxies</a> interact as well as the general <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/overview/\">evolution of our universe</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> backwards rainbow", "credit": "X-ray: <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>/<a href=\"https://cxc.harvard.edu/\">CXC</a>/<a href=\"https://info.ira.inaf.it/en/\">INAF-IRA</a>/<a href=\"https://people.ira.inaf.it/botteon/\">A. Botteon</a> <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.06659\">et al.</a>; Infrared: <a href=\"http://www.ukidss.org/\">UKIDSS</a>-<a href=\"http://www.ukidss.org/surveys/gps/gps.html\">GPS</a>; Radio: <a href=\"https://www.astron.nl/\">ASTRON</a><a href=\"https://www.astron.nl/telescopes/lofar/\">LOFAR</a>; Image processing: <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>/<a href=\"https://cxc.harvard.edu/\">CXC</a>/<a href=\"https://sao.si.edu/\">SAO</a>/<a href=\"https://www.cfa.harvard.edu/people/nancy-ra-wolk\">N. Wolk</a>", "copyright": "X-ray: <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>/<a href=\"https://cxc.harvard.edu/\">CXC</a>/<a href=\"https://info.ira.inaf.it/en/\">INAF-IRA</a>/<a href=\"https://people.ira.inaf.it/botteon/\">A. Botteon</a> <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.06659\">et al.</a>; Infrared: <a href=\"http://www.ukidss.org/\">UKIDSS</a>-<a href=\"http://www.ukidss.org/surveys/gps/gps.html\">GPS</a>; Radio: <a href=\"https://www.astron.nl/\">ASTRON</a><a href=\"https://www.astron.nl/telescopes/lofar/\">LOFAR</a>; Image processing: <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>/<a href=\"https://cxc.harvard.edu/\">CXC</a>/<a href=\"https://sao.si.edu/\">SAO</a>/<a href=\"https://www.cfa.harvard.edu/people/nancy-ra-wolk\">N. Wolk</a>", "alt": "See Explanation.  Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.", "url": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-19-treble-clef-unusual-x-ray-hot-radio-source/", "hdurl": "https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/trebleclef.jpg?w=7200&h=5361&fit=clip&crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint", "basic_html": "<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">\n<meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\">\n<title> APOD: 2026 October 19 - Treble Clef: Unusual X-ray Hot Radio Source</title>\n<link rel=\"canonical\" href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-19-treble-clef-unusual-x-ray-hot-radio-source/\">\n<!-- gsfc meta tags -->\n<meta name=\"orgcode\" content=\"661\">\n<meta name=\"rno\" content=\"Dana Bolles\">\n<meta name=\"content-owner\" content=\"Robert Nemiroff\">\n<meta name=\"webmaster\" content=\"Stephen.F.Fantasia.1\">\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"Explanation: Explanation : What is Treble Clef? This unusual radio structure extends over 2 million light-years and resembles a common musical symbol -- but has its visible light hidden by our own Milky Way Galaxy. Examined in 2004, the nature of VLSS J0318.9+5755, dubbed Treble Clef, remained unknown. Recent observations by the Chandra X-ray Observatory and eROSITA, however, have led to the hypothesis that the Treble Clef central cloud of charged particles was originally expelled by a supermassive black hole at the center of a galaxy. After the radio emission from those particles faded, they may have been reinvigorated by a shock wave from the collision of two clusters of galaxies. The featured composite image shows radio emission in red, while recently discovered X-rays are shown in blue. Unusual objects like Treble Clef give humanity unexpected clues about how whole clusters of galaxies interact as well as the general evolution of our universe.Tomorrow&#039;s picture: backwards rainbow\">\n<!-- -->\n<meta name=\"keywords\" content=\"treble clef: unusual x-ray hot radio source\">\n<meta property=\"og:type\" content=\"article\">\n<meta property=\"og:title\" content=\"Treble Clef: Unusual X-ray Hot Radio Source\">\n<meta property=\"og:url\" content=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-19-treble-clef-unusual-x-ray-hot-radio-source/\">\n<meta property=\"og:site_name\" content=\"NASA Science\">\n<meta property=\"og:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/trebleclef.jpg?w=7200&#038;h=5361&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:secure_url\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/trebleclef.jpg?w=7200&#038;h=5361&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n<meta property=\"og:image:alt\" content=\"See Explanation.  Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\">\n<meta property=\"article:published_time\" content=\"2026-10-19T00:05:00-04:00\">\n<meta property=\"article:modified_time\" content=\"2026-10-18T12:45:02-04:00\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:card\" content=\"summary_large_image\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:title\" content=\"Treble Clef: Unusual X-ray Hot Radio Source\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:site\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:creator\" content=\"@NASA\">\n<meta name=\"twitter:image\" content=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/trebleclef.jpg?w=7200&#038;h=5361&#038;fit=clip&#038;crop=faces%2Cfocalpoint\">\n</head>\n<body BGCOLOR=\"#F4F4FF\" text=\"#000000\" link=\"#0000FF\" vlink=\"#7F0F9F\"\nalink=\"#FF0000\">\n\n<center>\n<h1> Astronomy Picture of the Day </h1>\n<p>\n\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Discover the cosmos!</a>\nEach day a different image or photograph of our fascinating universe is\nfeatured, along with a brief explanation written by a professional astronomer.\n<p>\n\n2026 October 19<br>\n\n<a href=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/trebleclef.jpg\">\n<IMG SRC=\"https://assets.science.nasa.gov/dynamicimage/assets/science/cds/apod/apod/2026/october/trebleclef.jpg\" alt=\"See Explanation.  Clicking on the picture will download the highest resolution version available.\"></a>\n</center>\n\n<center>\n<b> Treble Clef: Unusual X-ray Hot Radio Source </b> <br>\n\n<b>Image Credit &\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Copyright</a>:</b>\n\nX-ray: <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>/<a href=\"https://cxc.harvard.edu/\">CXC</a>/<a href=\"https://info.ira.inaf.it/en/\">INAF-IRA</a>/<a href=\"https://people.ira.inaf.it/botteon/\">A. Botteon</a> <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.06659\">et al.</a>; Infrared: <a href=\"http://www.ukidss.org/\">UKIDSS</a>-<a href=\"http://www.ukidss.org/surveys/gps/gps.html\">GPS</a>; Radio: <a href=\"https://www.astron.nl/\">ASTRON</a><a href=\"https://www.astron.nl/telescopes/lofar/\">LOFAR</a>; Image processing: <a href=\"https://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a>/<a href=\"https://cxc.harvard.edu/\">CXC</a>/<a href=\"https://sao.si.edu/\">SAO</a>/<a href=\"https://www.cfa.harvard.edu/people/nancy-ra-wolk\">N. Wolk</a>\n</center>\n\n<p>\n\n<b> Explanation: </b>\n\n<strong>Explanation:</strong> <b>Explanation</b> : What is Treble Clef? This unusual radio structure extends over 2 million light-years and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pink_Panther_(character)#/media/File:Pink_Panther.png\">resembles</a> a common <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Clef#Treble_clef\">musical symbol</a> -- but has its <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/09_visiblelight/\">visible light</a> hidden by our own <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/resource/the-milky-way-galaxy/\">Milky Way Galaxy</a>. Examined in 2004, the nature of VLSS J0318.9+5755, <a href=\"https://chandra.si.edu/photo/2026/trebleclef/trebleclef_BU.mp4\">dubbed Treble Clef</a>, remained unknown. Recent observations by the <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/mission/chandra/\">Chandra X-ray Observatory</a> and <a href=\"https://www.mpe.mpg.de/eROSITA\">eROSITA</a>, however, have led to the <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.06659\">hypothesis</a> that the Treble Clef central cloud of charged particles was originally expelled by a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/asset/webb/supermassive-black-holes/\">supermassive black hole</a> at the center of a galaxy. After the radio emission from those particles faded, they may have been <a href=\"https://spacescoop.org/ar/scoops/1540/rise-of-the-radio-phoenix/\">reinvigorated</a> by a <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-february-16-unexplained-shocks-around-a-white-dwarf-star/\">shock wave</a> from the collision of two <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2024-march-27-the-coma-cluster-of-galaxies/\">clusters of galaxies</a>. The <a href=\"https://chandra.si.edu/photo/2026/trebleclef/\">featured composite image</a> shows <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/ems/05_radiowaves/\">radio</a> emission in red, while recently discovered <a href=\"https://chandra.harvard.edu/xray_astro/xrays.html\">X-rays</a> are shown in blue. <a href=\"https://unsplash.com/photos/santa-claus-with-red-scarf-7sgPfAcM6_o\">Unusual objects</a> like <a href=\"https://chandra.si.edu/photo/2026/trebleclef/trebleclef_dragon.mp4\">Treble Clef</a> give humanity unexpected clues about how whole <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/apod-2026-july-26-simulation-tng50-a-galaxy-cluster-forms/\">clusters of galaxies</a> interact as well as the general <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/universe/overview/\">evolution of our universe</a>.<br><br><strong>Tomorrow's picture:</strong> backwards rainbow\n<p> <center>\n\n\n<p> <hr>\n<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-18-sonified-eagle-nebula-pillars/\">&lt;</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Archive</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Index</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/archive/\">Search</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/calendar/\">Calendar</a>\n| <a href=\"/feed/apod-basic/\">RSS</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/education-links/\">Education</a>\n| <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">About APOD</a>\n| &gt;\n\n<hr><p>\n<b> Authors & editors: </b>\nRobert Nemiroff, Jerry Bonnell, Cecilia Chirenti, Keighley Rockcliffe<br>\n<b>NASA Official: </b> Dana Bolles<a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/apod/about-apod/\">Specific rights apply</a>.<br>\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/about/highlights/HP_Privacy.html\">NASA Web\nPrivacy Policy and Important Notices</a><br>\n<b>A service of:</b>\n<a href=\"http://astrophysics.gsfc.nasa.gov/\">ASD</a> at\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/\">NASA</a> /\n<a href=\"http://www.nasa.gov/centers/goddard/\">GSFC</a>\n<br><b>&</b> <a href=\"http://www.mtu.edu/\">Michigan Tech. U.</a><br>\n</center>\n</body>\n</html>", "basic_html_url": "https://science.nasa.gov/wp-json/wp/v2/apod-basic/261019/html"}